      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to set up a system of linear equations based on the given information. Let \\( p \\) be the cost of a pen in cents and \\( q \\) be the cost of a pencil in cents. We are given two pieces of information:\n\n1. Four pens and three pencils cost $2.24, which is equivalent to 224 cents.\n2. Two pens and five pencils cost $1.54, which is equivalent to 154 cents.\n\nWe can translate these pieces of information into the following system of equations:\n\\[\n4p + 3q = 224\n\\]\n\\[\n2p + 5q = 154\n\\]\n\nTo eliminate one of the variables, we can use the method of elimination. First, we will eliminate \\( p \\) by making the coefficients of \\( p \\) in both equations the same. We can do this by multiplying the second equation by 2:\n\\[\n2(2p + 5q) = 2(154)\n\\]\n\\[\n4p + 10q = 308\n\\]\n\nNow we have the system:\n\\[\n4p + 3q = 224\n\\]\n\\[\n4p + 10q = 308\n\\]\n\nNext, we subtract the first equation from the second equation to eliminate \\( p \\):\n\\[\n(4p + 10q) - (4p + 3q) = 308 - 224\n\\]\n\\[\n4p + 10q - 4p - 3q = 84\n\\]\n\\[\n7q = 84\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( q \\), we get:\n\\[\nq = \\frac{84}{7} = 12\n\\]\n\nSo, the cost of a pencil is 12 cents. To verify, we can find the cost of a pen by substituting \\( q = 12 \\) back into one of the original equations. We will use the first equation:\n\\[\n4p + 3(12) = 224\n\\]\n\\[\n4p + 36 = 224\n\\]\n\\[\n4p = 224 - 36\n\\]\n\\[\n4p = 188\n\\]\n\\[\np = \\frac{188}{4} = 47\n\\]\n\nSo, the cost of a pen is 47 cents. To check our solution, we substitute \\( p = 47 \\) and \\( q = 12 \\) into the second original equation:\n\\[\n2(47) + 5(12) = 94 + 60 = 154\n\\]\n\nThe values satisfy both equations, confirming our solution. Therefore, the cost of a pencil is \\(\\boxed{12}\\) cents.",
